- sum_prefix_scores(["abcd"])
- sum_prefix_scores(["a", "a", "a"])
- sum_prefix_scores(["ab", "ac", "ad"])
- sum_prefix_scores(["xyz", "xy", "x", "y"])
- sum_prefix_scores(["ab" * 15] * 300)[0]
fail[2]: With one word, each of its 4 prefixes has a score of 1.
fail[3]: `"a"` is a prefix of all 3 words, so each word scores 3.
fail[4]: `"a"` is a prefix of all 3 words (3), and each whole word is a prefix of itself only (1): 4.
fail[6]: All 300 words are the same, so each of the 30 prefixes scores 300. Checking every prefix against every word takes too long here.
error[TimeoutError]: Checking every prefix against every word takes too long. Count all the prefixes of all the words in one dictionary first, then add up the counts.
edges:
- one word :: len(words) == 1 :: sum_prefix_scores(["hey"])
- the same word twice :: len(set(words)) < len(words) :: sum_prefix_scores(["ab", "ab"])
- no shared first letters :: len(words) > 1 and len(set(w[0] for w in words)) == len(words) :: sum_prefix_scores(["ab", "cd"])
edge_count: 2
hint: Count every prefix of every word in one dictionary: `w[:i]` for `i` from 1 to `len(w)`.
hint: A prefix's count is the number of words that start with it, which is its score.
hint: Then, for each word, add up the counts of its own prefixes.
right: One dictionary holds the score of every prefix that appears, so each word's total is a sum of lookups instead of a comparison with every other word.
wrong: Count `w[:i]` for every word and every `i` from 1 to `len(w)`; then for each word, add up `counts[w[:i]]` over its prefixes.
